# Performance

## Objective

Perform well under realistic workloads while staying clean and maintainable. Optimize intelligently  -  not prematurely.

## Measure Before Optimizing

Identify bottlenecks with evidence. Do not sacrifice readability for theoretical gains.

## Prioritize Algorithms

Better algorithms beat micro-optimizations. Reduce unnecessary work: duplicate queries, repeated parsing, redundant loops.

## Database Performance
> **If this project has no database surface:** apply the same principles to the nearest persistent equivalent (files, config, key-value stores, local caches) or skip.

Prefer indexed lookups, pagination, selective columns, batching. Avoid N+1 patterns and `SELECT *` when inappropriate.

## API Performance
> **If this project has no HTTP/REST API surface:** apply the same principles to the nearest public interface (module APIs, SDK callbacks, CLI commands, RPC, message contracts) or skip.

Minimize duplicate network calls; use pagination, filtering, caching where appropriate.

## Memory and Collections

Avoid unbounded growth, unnecessary copies, and retained references. Release resources when done.

## Caching

Cache expensive, stable work  -  with clear invalidation. Cross-link: `caching-strategy.mdc`.

## Concurrency

Parallelize independent work when safe. If the project defines a thread or concurrency contract (see project workflow rules such as `vibecoding-cost-efficient.mdc`), honor it.

## Readability First

Do not make code significantly harder to understand for tiny gains.

## Documentation

Update documentation in `/docs` when the change is significant (see `documentation-first.mdc`).

## Golden Rule

Optimize where it matters; keep everything else simple.